From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Jonathan Schwender <schwenderjonathan@gmail.com>
Date: Thu, 15 Oct 2026 11:06:27 +0000
Subject: [PATCH] media (ohos): Report AVPlayer errors to the media element

The OpenHarmony AVPlayer backend never connected the AVPlayer error
callback, so a resource that AVPlayer can't play left the media element
in HAVE_NOTHING without an error: it never moved on to its next source
candidate, and it kept delaying the load event. Forward AVPlayer errors
as PlayerEvent::Error, as the GStreamer backend does with its errors.
Report the error state of AVPlayer as an error too, as some failures
only change the state, for example a failing play() or pause(). After
an error, ignore the info events of AVPlayer: it can still change
state, for example to paused after an I/O error, which would raise the
ready state of the failed element.

AVPlayer also reports operations that it does not permit, for example
a playback speed for media it treats as a live stream, which includes
media without a duration, or an audio interruption. These don't end
playback, so ignore them: a failure that does also moves AVPlayer to
its error state.

Fork note: meant for upstream. Drop once upstream has it.

Testing: resource-selection-candidate-moved hung on OpenHarmony; see
Validation.

Validation: a HiHope DAYU200 (OpenHarmony 7.0, API 26), headless release builds (`./mach build --ohos --headless-binary`) of ohos-main with patches 68 to 73, with and without this patch. `./mach test-wpt --ohos --processes 1` of media-elements/loading-the-media-resource and media-elements/error-codes (52 tests) plus the series' ten media tests: without this patch, 14 tests hang (load-events-networkState, resource-selection-invoke-insert-source-networkState, and the resource-selection-candidate-* and resource-selection-pointer-* tests); with it, every test matches its expectation with SpiderMonkey and with QuickJS, except autoplay-default-permissions-policy, which is OK against its TIMEOUT expectation as on Linux. Some failures now fire error where elements used to wait forever: (1) AVPlayer reports VID_DEC_ERR when the board runs too many H.264 decoders, for example with two processes that each autoplay 8 videos: without this patch the failed elements stay in HAVE_NOTHING without an event and the others stall, with it they fire error. With `--processes 2`, gc-unreferenced-media-element and autoplay each failed this way once, now that they can overlap with other video tests (the candidate-moved hang used to keep the other process busy); this backend also never releases an AVPlayer (a pre-existing reference cycle), so players of earlier tests in the same process may add to the load. (2) The system media_service crashed in its video decoder pipeline several times during these runs (also before this patch); AVPlayer then reports "mediaserver is died" for every player, which now fires error, and rejects play() for elements that load from a src attribute and have no metadata yet. Later re-validation, with the error state reported and not permitted operations ignored: the same 60 tests match their expectations with SpiderMonkey and with QuickJS, and a Vorbis WebM muxed for streaming, which AVPlayer treats as a live stream, loads and resumes after pause() without an error event.
AI-assisted: Yes (agent session; written and tested by the agent)
Signed-off-by: Jonathan Schwender <schwenderjonathan@gmail.com>
